        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Human spatial orientation normally relies upon signals from the inner ear to indicate the vertical position.  During the exposure to microgravity this process undergoes adaptive changes.  The D-1 investigation extended a previous experiment flown on Spacelab-1 (STS-9) by use of linear acceleration stimuli using the ESA Vestibular Sled, and visual stimuli provided by the Rotating Dome apparatus.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
The Sled is a rail-mounted linear acceleration sled, used to accelerate subjects along a track in the lateral upright (Y-axis) and longitudinal supine (Z-axis) orientations.  The sled consists of a chair with an attached head restraint.  The chair is mounted on a cart which is guided along two rails and pulled by a cable.  The total travel distance is 3.2 meters.  Different sinusoidal and constant acceleration motion profiles, ranging from 0.001-G to 0.2-G, can be chosen.  The head restraint is equipped with a visor-helmet to blind the test subject to avoid visual cues.
<P>
The test protocol included measurements of the ability to detect small linear acceleration steps, perceive motion path, and the performance of the closed loop otolith assessment test (CLOAT).  During the D-1 mission the first two protocols were performed preflight, inflight on the first and sixth day of the seven day mission, and postflight 1, 2, and 10 days after landing.  The CLOAT task was performed pre- and postflight only.
<P>
The ability to detect near-threshold acceleration steps was measured by the time delay between the motion-stimulus and the subjective response.  Thirteen different acceleration profiles, ranging from 0.001-G to 0.08-G were presented in a random order, one for each acceleration level.  The subject was instructed to indicate the direction of subjective acceleration using a hand-held joystick.  The time delay between actual and indicated change in acceleration was measured as the time-to-detect.  Tests were valued as incorrect responses when the subject indicated acceleration in the opposite direction than the actual movement, or when the subject did not respond at all.  Electrooculography (EOG) was used to measure lateral and vertical eye movements in response to both lateral (Y-axis) and longitudinal (Z-axis) sinusoidal accelerations. Ocular torsion (rotations about the visual axis) was measured during sinusoidal accelerations (rotation between 0.2 and 0.8 Hertz, 0.2-G) using a photographic technique. 
<P>
The CLOAT task was performed only pre- and postflight using the U.S. Laboratory Sled, which was conceptually and functionally similar to the ESA sled, but performed under 1-G conditions.  It allowed controlled acceleration up to 0.7-G with a useable track length of 4.7 meters.  During the CLOAT test the subject was in control of the sled velocity using a joystick.  A disturbance motion was generated and the subject was asked to try to nullify the disturbance motion and hold the cart still.  The CLOAT test was performed once with open eyes and three times with eyes closed for each test session. The results of the sled velocity profiles of the three darkened runs were averaged for analysis.
<P>
The rotating dome apparatus consisted of a closed hollow cylinder into which the subject looked.  The dome surface filled the subject’s field of view and its inside was covered randomly with multi-colored dots.  A video camera lens was mounted in the center of the dome (rotation axis) to monitor ocular torsion.  The subject  was asked to focus at infinity as much as possible during dome rotation and indicate subjective vection intensity with the right hand using a spring-loaded joystick attached to the stationary dome frame.  The dome could be rotated clockwise and counterclockwise at selected speeds.  The inflight trials consisted of two conditions: “freefloat” and “tactile”.  For the freefloat condition the subject was restrained only by the use of a custom-fit biteboard.  For the tractile portion the subject was held to a rigid floor surface by stretched elastic cords from a shoulder harness, producing localized tactile forces on the feet comparable to the forces felt while standing in 1-G on Earth.  The pre- and postflight tests were done in standing and supine positions.
<P>
Experiment protocols were broken down into “dome runs”. Each dome run for any given condition consisted of a 6 minute computer-controlled sequence of six trails in randomized order, with dome-rotation speeds of 30, 45, and 60 degrees per second. Each trial had 50 seconds of dome rotation, and was separated from the next by a 10 second pause.  The subjects kept their head in the dome for the entire run (6 trials), but came out of the dome between runs.  During ground tests, each subject performed two dome runs of the same test condition in each session. Inflight, a free-floating run and a tactile run performed within a few minutes of each other constituted a session.  Inflight tests were scheduled for Mission Days 0, 2, 3, and 4 of the D-1 mission.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
The time-to-detect small acceleration steps was consistently lower under microgravity conditions than on the ground.  Thereby, the results were the same independent of the subject or the subject’s position during the test (sitting or supine). The time-to-detect was particularly short during the transition periods from 1-G to micro-G and from micro-G to 1-G, as measured during the first inflight test session on the first flight day, and on the first day after landing, indicating a hypersensitivity which caused a more erratic response.  
<P>
The threshold for detection of linear accelerations was defined as the lowest level at which three out of four responses at that level and above were correct.  During preflight data collection significant differences between the test subjects concerning their individual threshold and its range were measured.  All test subjects exhibited a reduction of the threshold between early and late inflight tests (Flight Day 1 and FD 5) for the Y-axis tests (lateral upright), and a constant or increasing threshold for the Z-axis tests (longitudinal supine).
<P>
After riding the sled with blinded eyes under micrograivity conditions, the subjects were asked how they perceived the motion.  The subjects reported that their perceptions (while riding the sled) were qualitatively the same in space as on the ground.  One subject described perceiving a “hilltop illusion”, like riding over a small hill.
<P>
The performance in the CLOAT task performed in the light showed little change between preflight and postflight results.  In the dark all subjects showed enhanced performance postflight in both the Y- and Z-axis.  Overall, the results from this D-1 investigation confirm earlier findings that astronauts return to Earth more capable of sensing and reacting to linear accelerations than preflight.   
<P>
During the tests with the Rotating dome, the subjects were asked to describe their sensations.  Four out of five subjects indicated that they experienced saturated vection at some time during the flight trials.  Strong differences were noted during the free-floating and tactile conditions to the effect that the tactile cues reduced the intensity and consistency of vection.  Finally, it was specifically mentioned that the inflight free-float condition produced vection which was stronger than that in any of the preflight dome tests, including those in the supine position.  
<P>
Because the vection intensity is an individual, subjective sensation, the data collected from each subject was analyzed separately.  The three analyzed parameters were maximum vection, time to saturation, and total time of saturation.  All subjects developed an increased vection intensity in microgravity, thereby the results from the first mission day were between the values of the preflight tests and the results achieved later during the mission. Vection consistency was generally higher in the free-float condition.
<P>
The increased vection intensity inflight indicates an increased reliance upon motion cues in microgravity compared to ground tests.  On Mission Days 2, 3, and 4, the subjects nearly always reached complete saturated vection on each run, a phenomenon almost never observed preflight -- even in the supine postion where otolith cues would not be expected to inhibit roll vection.  The increase reliance upon visual cues for spatial orientation, as evidenced by vection strength, apparently develops gradually during the course of adaptation to microgravity, but is largely complete by the third day in orbit if not earlier.
        </div>

